# Lab 05 - Destination NAT (Port Forwarding) on OPNsense
## Objective
The objective of this lab is to understand how Destination Network Address Translation (DNAT), also known as Port Forwarding, works in OPNsense.
In this lab, a Python HTTP server running on a Kali Linux machine located on the LAN network is published through the WAN interface of OPNsense. An Ubuntu client on the WAN network accesses the web server using the firewall's WAN IP address, demonstrating how OPNsense translates the destination address and forwards incoming traffic to the internal server.
## Network Topology

The lab environment consists of one OPNsense firewall, one Kali Linux machine hosting a web server, one Ubuntu machine acting as the client, and a Windows host running VMware Workstation.

The WAN interface of OPNsense is connected to **VMnet8 (NAT)**, allowing communication with the Ubuntu virtual machine. The LAN interface is connected to **VMnet1 (Host-Only)**, where the Kali Linux server is located.

The objective is to publish the HTTP service running on Kali to the WAN network using a Destination NAT (Port Forwarding) rule.

