## Prerequisites

Before configuring Destination NAT (Port Forwarding), the following conditions were verified:

- The Python HTTP server was running on the Kali Linux machine.
- The Ubuntu virtual machine was configured as the client to test connectivity from the WAN network.
- Network connectivity between the firewall interfaces and both virtual machines was verified.
## Step 1: Start the HTTP Server on Kali Linux

A simple HTTP server was started on the Kali Linux machine using Python. This server listens on TCP port **8080** and acts as the internal web service that will be published through OPNsense using Destination NAT.

bash
python3 -m http.server 8080
```

After executing the command, the server listens on all available interfaces.

text
Serving HTTP on 0.0.0.0 port 8080

## Step 2: Configure the Ubuntu Test Client

The Ubuntu virtual machine was connected to the **VMware VMnet8 (NAT)** network to simulate an external client located on the WAN side of the firewall.

The Ubuntu machine obtained the following IP address:

| Machine | IP Address | Network |
|---------|------------|---------|
| Ubuntu | 192.168.152.132 | VMnet8 (WAN) |

This client was later used to verify that HTTP requests sent to the OPNsense WAN interface were successfully forwarded to the internal Kali Linux web server.
## Step 3: Configure the Destination NAT Rule

A Destination NAT (Port Forwarding) rule was created to allow HTTP traffic arriving at the WAN interface of OPNsense to be forwarded to the internal web server running on Kali Linux.

The rule forwards TCP traffic received on port **8080** of the WAN interface to the Kali Linux server listening on the same port.

The following configuration was applied:

| Parameter | Value |
|-----------|-------|
| Interface | WAN |
| Protocol | TCP |
| Destination | WAN Address |
| Destination Port | 8080 |
| Redirect Target IP | 192.168.105.19 |
| Redirect Target Port | 8080 |
| Filter Rule Association | Pass |

After creating the rule, the configuration was saved and applied.
### Destination NAT Traffic Flow

When the Ubuntu client sends an HTTP request to the WAN interface of OPNsense, the firewall checks the Destination NAT rules.

If the destination matches the configured rule, OPNsense replaces the destination IP address with the internal IP address of the Kali Linux server before forwarding the packet.


Before Destination NAT

Source      : 192.168.152.132
Destination : 192.168.152.130:8080

After Destination NAT
Source      : 192.168.152.132
Destination : 192.168.105.19:8080

Only the destination address is translated. The source address remains unchanged because the client identity must be preserved.

## Step 4: Verify the Destination NAT Configuration

After applying the Destination NAT rule, the Ubuntu virtual machine was used to verify that external clients could successfully access the internal web server.

The Ubuntu machine, connected to the WAN network, opened a web browser and accessed the following URL:

http://192.168.152.130:8080

The request was sent to the WAN interface of OPNsense. The firewall matched the Destination NAT rule and forwarded the request to the internal Kali Linux web server.

The browser successfully displayed the Python HTTP server directory listing, confirming that the Destination NAT rule was working correctly.
### Packet Flow Analysis

The HTTP request followed the path below:


Ubuntu Client
192.168.152.132
        │
        │ HTTP Request
        ▼
OPNsense WAN
192.168.152.130
        │
        │ Destination NAT
        ▼
Kali Linux
192.168.105.19


When the packet arrived at the WAN interface, OPNsense evaluated the Destination NAT rules and found a matching rule for TCP port **8080**.

The firewall replaced the destination IP address from **192.168.152.130** to **192.168.105.19** while preserving the original source IP address (**192.168.152.132**).

The request was then forwarded to the Kali Linux HTTP server, which generated the HTTP response and returned it to the client through OPNsense.
